      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Blend the tinned tomatoes, red peppers, and half the onion into a smooth puree."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat oil in a large pot over medium-high heat. Fry the sliced onion for 5 minutes until starting to colour."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add tomato puree and stir fry for 2 minutes. Pour in the blended tomato mixture."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cook over high heat for 20-25 minutes, stirring every few minutes, until the sauce is thick, dry, and the oil has separated to the surface."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add thyme, bay leaves, salt, and pepper. Pour in the stock and bring to the boil."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the washed rice and stir well. Reduce heat, cover tightly with foil then a lid, and cook on very low heat for 30 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Rest 10 minutes without lifting the lid. Fluff gently and serve."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Rice has cooked unevenly with wet parts and dry parts",
              "cause": "Not sealed tightly enough or heat uneven",
              "fix": "Ensure foil is tight under the lid and place a folded cloth over the lid to retain heat"
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Jollof tastes very sour",
              "cause": "Tomato base not cooked long enough",
              "fix": "The oil must separate to the top and the sauce must look dark and dry before adding rice"
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Rice is sticking to the bottom of the pot",
              "cause": "Heat too high during the steaming stage",
              "fix": "This is actually called socoroto and is the prized smoky layer - take care not to burn it past golden"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Ghana and Nigeria have a long-running and spirited debate about whose jollof rice is better. The Ghanaian version is typically made with long-grain or basmati rice, tends to be slightly less oily, and the tomato sauce is cooked down until deeply concentrated. It is a national dish served at every Ghanaian celebration from funerals to weddings. The argument with Nigeria continues indefinitely."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
